          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had reflux for years and now food sometimes sticks going down. How worrying is that, and what does guidance describe about when to investigate?",
          "answer": "Your symptom of food sticking while swallowing, alongside a history of reflux, is assessed as routine. This means it does not represent an immediate emergency. However, if any symptoms worsen or become severe or if there are new unexplained symptoms, it is advisable to seek medical advice.\n\nGuidelines indicate that when assessing esophageal symptoms like food sticking, it is crucial to evaluate for \"alarm features.\" Alarm features may include difficulty swallowing, unintentional weight loss, or gastrointestinal bleeding, and if these are present, further investigation may be warranted. In your case, discussing your symptoms with a healthcare provider would help determine whether further evaluation, such as an endoscopy, is necessary to understand your condition better.\n\nFor individuals with reflux who develop concerning symptoms, it is essential for clinicians to consider the possibility of requiring additional investigations, particularly if alarm features are present. Initial management approaches for gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) may vary, but further evaluation is suggested when significant warning signs arise.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
